{"id":"c02ef390-0704-40ed-8250-3b3d7d9c952f","object":"chat.completion","created":1784808601,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\\n    passes = case[\\\"passes\\\"]\\n    max_rounds = case[\\\"max_rounds\\\"]\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        new_text = text\\n        for p in passes:\\n            new_text = new_text.replace(p[\\\"old\\\"], p[\\\"new\\\"])\\n        if new_text == text:\\n            break\\n        text = new_text\\n    return text\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to generate a JSON object with key \"implementation\", value being Python source code defining function solve(case). The function must return JSON-serializable object. Based on the task: \"Registered procedure task SE-CR-01\". The fixture shows cases with text, passes (list of transformations each with old and new strings), max_rounds. The task likely involves applying transformations repeatedly in rounds until no more changes or max_rounds reached. The name \"halve-a\" etc suggest replacing old with new. But note \"drop-c\" has old \" cc\" and new \"\" meaning remove \" cc\". So we need to simulate multiple passes: each pass apply each transformation in order? The problem statement says \"Registered procedure task SE-CR-01\" but no further details. From the fixture, we need to infer the function. Possibly the task is to simulate the effect of applying the passes sequentially each round, for max_rounds rounds, and return final text? But the fixture payload includes \"_case_nonce\" which is likely for verification but not needed for solve. The function must not mutate case. So we should return a result.\n\nGiven lack of explicit instructions beyond the fixture, we need to guess the intended behavior. The title \"EFFECTSLICE_MODEL_VISIBLE_PAYLOAD_V1\" and \"task_scaffold:429\" suggest this is a coding challenge. Often such tasks ask to implement a string rewriting system. The passes array: each pass is a transformation: replace old with new. But note the passes are named, but we apply them in order. In each round, we go through passes and replace all occurrences? Or only one replacement? Probably replace all occurrences of old with new for that pass, then next pass. And rounds continue until no changes in a round or max_rounds reached. That is common.\n\nCheck first case: text \"aaaa bb cc\". Passes: halve-a: \"aa\" -> \"a\", drop-b: \"bb\" -> \"b\", drop-c: \" cc\" -> \"\". If we apply in order round 1: start \"aaaa bb cc\". Apply halve-a: replace all \"aa\" with \"a\": \"aa\" becomes \"a\", so \"aaaa\" becomes \"aa\" then \"a\"? Actually careful: \"aaaa\" has two overlapping \"aa\"? Typically replace leftmost non-overlapping. But Python's str.replace replaces all non-overlapping occurrences from left to right. So \"aaaa\".replace(\"aa\",\"a\") -> \"aa\" because first two \"aa\" become \"a\", leaving third and fourth \"aa\"? Actually \"aaaa\" length 4. Replace \"aa\" with \"a\": first occurrence positions 0-1 becomes \"a\", then remaining positions 2-3 become \"a\", result \"aa\". Then apply drop-b: \"bb\" -> \"b\": text now \"aa bb cc\" -> \"aa b cc\"? Actually after first pass: \"aa bb cc\". Then replace \"bb\" -> \"b\": becomes \"aa b cc\". Then drop-c: \" cc\" -> \"\": becomes \"aa b\". So round1 result \"aa b\". Round2: apply passes again: halve-a: \"aa\" -> \"a\", so \"aa b\" -> \"a b\". drop-b: \"b\" -> \"b\" (no change because \"bb\" not present). drop-c: \" cc\" not present. So result \"a b\". Round3: halve-a: no \"aa\", no changes. So final \"a b\". But max_rounds=8, so output \"a b\". That seems plausible.\n\nBut we need to return JSON-serializable object. Could return a dict with \"final_text\" or just the string. The problem statement didn't specify output format. Likely the expected output is the final text after processing. However, the fixture shows only input shapes. There might be expected outputs elsewhere. But we must implement solve(case). Probably it should return the final text. But to be safe, we can return a dict with key \"result\" or just the string. The example fixtures don't show expected output. In many such challenges, the solve function returns the final string. I'll assume return the final text as a string.\n\nImplementation: The case is a dict with keys \"text\", \"passes\", \"max_rounds\". We'll iterate rounds, apply each pass in order, using str.replace for each. Continue until no change in a full round or rounds exceed max_rounds. Return final text.\n\nWe must not mutate case, so we copy the text.\n\nEdge cases: passes may have overlapping? But standard replace. Also max_rounds may be large.\n\nWe'll write Python source inside a string.\n\nThe output must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". The value is a string containing Python source. Need to escape newlines, quotes, backslashes. In JSON, we must encode the Python code as a JSON string. So we need to represent the code with proper escaping.\n\nLet's draft code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case[\"text\"]\n    passes = case[\"passes\"]\n    max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        new_text = text\n        for p in passes:\n            new_text = new_text.replace(p[\"old\"], p[\"new\"])\n        if new_text == text:\n            break\n        text = new_text\n    return text\n\nThat seems correct. But we need to ensure we don't mutate case. We only read.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable: string is fine.\n\nNow produce JSON.\n\nNote: The instruction says \"Return one JSON object with exactly one key, `implementation`. Its value must be Python source defining `solve(case)`.\" So the entire response must be that JSON object.\n\nWe must not use Markdown code fences. Just raw JSON.\n\nThus final output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\\n    passes = case[\\\"passes\\\"]\\n    max_rounds = case[\\\"max_rounds\\\"]\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        new_text = text\\n        for p in passes:\\n            new_text = new_text.replace(p[\\\"old\\\"], p[\\\"new\\\"])\\n        if new_text == text:\\n            break\\n        text = new_text\\n    return text\"}\n\nNeed to escape backslashes, double quotes, newlines properly. Newlines become \\n. Backslashes: none here. Double quotes inside: the string has double quotes around keys like \"text\". In JSON, we need to escape those inner double quotes by backslash. Also the string itself is enclosed in double quotes. So we need to escape all double quotes inside: \\\"text\\\" etc.\n\nLet's produce the escaped string.\n\nOriginal Python source:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case[\"text\"]\n    passes = case[\"passes\"]\n    max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        new_text = text\n        for p in passes:\n            new_text = new_text.replace(p[\"old\"], p[\"new\"])\n        if new_text == text:\n            break\n        text = new_text\n    return text\n\nNow escape for JSON: Replace every double quote with \\\", and every backslash with \\\\. Also encode newline as \\n. But we need to make sure the newlines are represented as literal \\n in JSON string. So the string will contain \\n characters.\n\nBetter to construct programmatically. I'll manually write the escaped string.\n\nLine by line:\n\nFirst line: \"def solve(case):\\n\"\nSecond: \"    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\\n\"\nThird: \"    passes = case[\\\"passes\\\"]\\n\"\nFourth: \"    max_rounds = case[\\\"max_rounds\\\"]\\n\"\nFifth: \"    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n\"\nSixth: \"        new_text = text\\n\"\nSeventh: \"        for p in passes:\\n\"\nEighth: \"            new_text = new_text.replace(p[\\\"old\\\"], p[\\\"new\\\"])\\n\"\nNinth: \"        if new_text == text:\\n\"\nTenth: \"            break\\n\"\nEleventh: \"        text = new_text\\n\"\nTwelfth: \"    return text\"\n\nCombine with newline after each, but no trailing newline? The string should contain newlines as needed. The final line has no newline at end. But it's fine.\n\nNow escape double quotes: In lines that have double quotes, replace \" with \\\". Lines:\n\"    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\" becomes \"    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\" but in JSON we need to escape, so the inner \\\" becomes \\\\\\\"? Actually careful: In JSON string, a double quote must be escaped as \\\". So the literal characters \\\" inside the string are written as \\\\\". For example, case[\"text\"] in source has double quotes. In JSON string, we need to represent: case[\\\"text\\\"] but backslash must be escaped. Actually we need two backslashes to get one backslash in the JSON string? No, the JSON string is a sequence of characters. A double quote character inside the string must be preceded by a backslash. So the representation is \\\". But in the JSON source, the backslash itself is a escape character, so we write \\\\\" to get a literal backslash followed by a quote? Wait:\n\nJSON string: \"hello \\\"world\\\"\". In the JSON text, you see backslash-doublequote. That means the string contains a backslash and a quote? No, the backslash is an escape, so it represents a single double quote character. So to have a literal backslash in the string, you need \\\\ (double backslash). So to represent the Python source text case[\"text\"], the JSON string must contain: case[\\\"text\\\"] but the backslashes are escape characters for the JSON parser, so they need to be escaped as \\\\\". So we need to write: case[\\\\\"text\\\\\"]? That would produce backslash then quote? Let's think step by step.\n\nWe want the Python source string to include a double quote character. In JSON, double quote characters in a string are represented as \\\". So if we have a double quote inside, we write \\\". But in the JSON representation, the backslash is an escape for the next character. So to include a literal double quote, we write \\\". So for example, the JSON string \"\\\"hello\\\"\" represents the string: \"hello\" (including quotes). So the JSON literal is: \"\\\"hello\\\"\". That's a sequence of characters: double quote, backslash, double quote, h,e,l,l,o, backslash, double quote, double quote. That's the JSON syntax.\n\nNow, we want to put the Python source code as a JSON string. In that source code, there are double quotes: case[\"text\"]. To have that double quote appear in the JSON string, we need to write \\\". So in the JSON string, we write: case[\\\"text\\\"]. That will be parsed as: c,a,s,e,[,\",t,e,x,t,\",]. That's what we want.\n\nBut then there is a complication: the backslash character itself. In JSON, backslash is also escaped as \\\\. So if we want a literal backslash in the string (e.g., for Python escape sequences like \\n), we need \\\\n? Actually, \\n in JSON represents newline character. But we want the Python source to contain a literal newline character (the newline between lines). In JSON, we can represent newline as \\n which will be interpreted as actual newline. So we should use \\n in the JSON string to represent newlines in the source code. That is fine because the newline character is part of the source.\n\nBut note: if we write newline as \\n, then the JSON string will contain that escape sequence, and when parsed, it becomes actual newline. That is correct for Python source code.\n\nNow, what about backslashes that are part of Python string literals? In our code, we don't have any backslashes except those we might introduce for escape sequences. So no extra escaping.\n\nThus, we can generate the JSON string by taking the Python source and:\n- replace each double quote with \\\"\n- replace each backslash with \\\\ (though none)\n- replace each newline with \\n\n- then wrap in double quotes.\n\nWe can do that manually.\n\nLet me write the Python source as a raw string (triple quotes) and then escape.\n\nBut I'll manually produce the escaped version.\n\nOriginal source (with newlines):\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case[\"text\"]\n    passes = case[\"passes\"]\n    max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        new_text = text\n        for p in passes:\n            new_text = new_text.replace(p[\"old\"], p[\"new\"])\n        if new_text == text:\n            break\n        text = new_text\n    return text\n\nNow produce JSON string:\n\n\"def solve(case):\\n    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\\n    passes = case[\\\"passes\\\"]\\n    max_rounds = case[\\\"max_rounds\\\"]\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        new_text = text\\n        for p in passes:\\n            new_text = new_text.replace(p[\\\"old\\\"], p[\\\"new\\\"])\\n        if new_text == text:\\n            break\\n        text = new_text\\n    return text\"\n\nCheck escaping: The double quotes inside are preceded by backslash. That should be correct.\n\nNow we need to wrap in object.\n\nFinal JSON:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\\n    passes = case[\\\"passes\\\"]\\n    max_rounds = case[\\\"max_rounds\\\"]\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        new_text = text\\n        for p in passes:\\n            new_text = new_text.replace(p[\\\"old\\\"], p[\\\"new\\\"])\\n        if new_text == text:\\n            break\\n        text = new_text\\n    return text\"}\n\nThat's it. We must ensure no extra whitespace before { or after }.\n\nAlso note the instruction \"Do not use Markdown code fences\". So plain JSON.\n\nI'll output that."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":717,"completion_tokens":3212,"total_tokens":3929,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":640},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3094},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":640,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":77},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}